.TH INTRO 5
.SH NAME
intro \- introduction to 9P2000.L as Vectra speaks it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B size[4] type[1] tag[2] ...
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
9P2000.L is the protocol every Vectra service speaks:
the kernel's own devices, the file servers that run as programs,
and the draw server behind
.BR /ws .
See
.IR intro (1)
for what a service is and how it is bound into a name space.
This page and the rest of section 5 describe the messages on the wire
between a client and a server.
.PP
Vectra adds nothing to 9P2000.L.
The version string is
.BR 9P2000.L ,
unchanged, and no message here carries a private field.
When a device needs an operation the protocol does not have,
the operation is a file:
a
.B ctl
file that takes a line of text, or a
.B status
file that renders state on a read.
See
.IR cons (3)
for the model.
.PP
Most of what runs in Vectra never sees a byte of this.
A kernel device and its caller both speak decoded messages,
an Odin union over the message bodies below,
and the codec that turns one into bytes runs only where an address
space is actually crossed: over a pipe to a program under
.BR /srv ,
or over the network eventually.
See
.IR vectra9 (2).
A read of
.B /dev/cons
costs one call and no encoding at all.
The wire this manual describes is what a program outside the kernel
actually reads and writes, and what
.IR mnt (9)
carries between the kernel and it.
.SS Encoding
Every message is
.B size[4]
.B type[1]
.B tag[2]
followed by a body particular to its kind.
.I Size
counts itself: it is the length in bytes of the whole message,
including the four bytes of the size field.
Integers are little-endian, least significant byte first.
A string is
.I len[2]
followed by
.I len
bytes of UTF-8 text, never NUL-terminated;
the notation
.B name[s]
in a SYNOPSIS means this shape.
A
.I qid
is thirteen bytes on the wire:
.B type[1]
.B version[4]
.BR path[8] .
.I Path
is the server's permanent identity for a file,
and two qids with the same path name the same file for as long as it exists.
.I Version
changes when the contents change.
.SS Message size
.B Tversion
negotiates
.IR msize ,
the largest single message either side will send.
8192 bytes is Vectra's default, matching what Linux's
.I v9fs
asks for.
.I Msize
bounds the wire, not an in-process call, where there is no buffer to
overflow.
A client chunks a bulk read or write by
.IR iounit ,
the number
.B Rlopen
or
.B Rlcreate
returns, never by
.IR msize .
.I Iounit
is
.I msize
less
.BR IOHDRSZ ,
24 bytes: the size of a
.BR Twrite 's
fixed fields, fid, offset and count, rounded up by one,
because a
.B Twrite
header is longer than the
.B Rread
that carries a read's answer back.
.SS Fids, tags and qids
A
.I fid
is a client's handle on a file, a 32-bit number the client chooses.
.B NOFID,
all bits set, means no fid: an attach with nothing to authenticate
against.
A
.I tag
names one outstanding request, chosen by the client.
.B NOTAG,
all bits set, is legal only on
.BR Tversion ,
before a tag space exists.
A
.I qid
is the server's own identity for a file, described above.
.SS Two rules that are easy to get wrong
A short
.B Rwalk
(fewer qids than the
.B Twalk
asked for)
is a success carrying a partial result, not an error.
Only a walk that fails on its first element is an error reply, and
.I newfid
is bound only when every element in the walk succeeded.
See
.IR walk (5).
.PP
.B Tflush
is not a cancellation.
It is a synchronisation point.
The server may finish the flushed request or abandon it,
but
.B Rflush
must follow whichever it does,
and the client must not reuse the tag until
.B Rflush
arrives.
See
.IR flush (5).
.SS Errors
Two error vocabularies exist, and they are not merged.
A message whose bytes are wrong, too short, of an unknown type,
or naming more walk elements than the protocol allows,
has no reply built for it at all: it is a failure of the transport
carrying it, not an answer from a server.
A message the server understood and refuses gets
.BR Rlerror ,
whose
.I ecode
field is a Linux errno.
See
.IR error (5).
